              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            structure on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} categories with weak equivalences
            is a model for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-category of (∞,1)-categories
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            presents under Dwyer-Kan
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} simplicial localization
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicially+enriched+category"} simplicially enriched category
            or alternatively under
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Rezk"} Charles Rezk
            %em simplicial nerve
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Segal+space"} Segal space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
            with the same objects of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and such that every weak equivalence in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            becomes a true equivalence (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"}> homotopy equivalence
            ) in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
          %p By the work of Barwick and Kan, there is a model structure on relative categories presenting the (∞,1)-category of simplicial spaces.
          %h2#details Details
          %p
            For the purposes of the present entry, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            means the bare minimum of what may reasonably go by that name:
          %p
            %strong Definition
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,W)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            equipped with a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wide+subcategory"} wide subcategory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          %p
            A morphism in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            is called a
            %strong weak equivalence
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} 2-out-of-3
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
          %p
            A morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C_1,W_1) \to (C_2,W_2)
            of relative catgeories is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1 \to C_2
            that preserves weak equivalences.
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi RelCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RelCat
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of relative categories and such morphisms between them.
          %h3#model_category_structure Model category structure
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The subdivided nerve of Barwick-Kan induces a right
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transferred+model+structure"} transferred model structure
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi RelCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RelCat
              such that there is a Quillen equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi ξ
                    %mo :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi sSet
                      %mi Reedy
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} op
                    %mo ⇆
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi RelCat
                    %mo ,
                    %mi BK
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi ξ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    K_\xi : sSet^{\Delta^{\op}}_{Reedy} \leftrightarrows (RelCat, BK) : N_\xi
            %p
              Both functors preserve and reflect weak equivalences, and the adjunction unit and counit are natural weak equivalences. The simplicial nerve
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              is naturally weakly equivalent to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mi ξ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_\xi
            %p
              Furthermore, this all remains true if the Reedy model structure on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi sSet
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet^{\Delta^{\op}}
              is replaced with any of its Bousfield localizations.
            %p
              The model structure on RelCat is left proper. It is also right proper when the model structure on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi sSet
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet^{\Delta^{\op}}
              is right proper.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The existence and properness of the model structure and the Quillen equivalence is Theorem 6.1 of
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#BarKan"} Barwick-Kan
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi ξ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_\xi
              preserves and reflects weak equivalences.
            %p
              Proposition 10.3 shows the adjunction unit is a natural weak equivalence. That the counit is a natural weak equivalence follows by considering the triangle identity, 3-for-2, and the fact
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi ξ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_\xi
              reflects weak equivalences
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi ξ
                    %mi C
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi η
                        %msub
                          %mi N
                          %mi ξ
                        %mi C
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi ξ
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi ξ
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi ξ
                    %mi C
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi N
                          %mi ξ
                        %mi ε
                        %mi C
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi ξ
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    N_\xi C \xrightarrow{\eta N_\xi C} N_\xi K_\xi N_\xi C
                    \\xrightarrow{N_\xi \varepsilon C} N_\xi C
            %p Finally, by considering the diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi η
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi N
                              %mi ξ
                            %msub
                              %mi K
                              %mi ξ
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi f
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi η
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi N
                              %mi ξ
                            %msub
                              %mi K
                              %mi ξ
                            %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp; \stackrel{\eta}{\to} &amp; N_\xi K_\xi X
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow f &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    Y &amp; \stackrel{\eta}{\to} &amp; N_\xi K_\xi Y
                    }
            %p
              we see
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a weak equivalence iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi ξ
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi ξ
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_\xi K_\xi f
              is, and iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi ξ
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_\xi f
              is.
          %p In particular, Rezk’s complete Segal space structure on bisimplicial sets is a Bousfield localization of the Reedy model structure, so we can define:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi RelCat
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Rezk
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (RelCat, Rezk)
              denote the model structure corresponding to the complete Segal spaces.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              The localization
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi RelCat
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Rezk
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(RelCat, Rezk) \simeq (\infty,1)Cat
              is the ∞-localization functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,W) \mapsto L(C, W)
              inverting the weak equivalences
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              We will see below that we can compute this through the map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi NC
                    %mo ,
                    %mi NW
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,W) \to (NC, NW)
              to marked simplicial sets. Suppose we’re given a fibrant replcaement
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi NC
                      %mo ,
                      %mi NW
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ♮
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (NC, NW) \to Y^\natural
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi NC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NC
              is a quasi-category, composition induces an equivalence for every quasi-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Fun
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi Map
                      %mo ♭
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ♮
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi Z
                      %mo ♮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Map
                      %mo ♭
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi NC
                    %mo ,
                    %mi NW
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi Z
                      %mo ♮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Fun
                      %mi NW
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi NC
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Fun(Y, Z) \simeq Map^\flat(Y^\natural, Z^\natural)
                    \\to Map^\flat((NC, NW), Z^\natural) \simeq Fun_{NW}(NC, Z)
            %p
              thus
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              satisfies the universal property of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi NC
                      %mo ,
                      %mi NW
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(NC, NW)
          %p
            It is shown in
            %a{:href => "#Meier"} Meier
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+fibrant+objects"} categories of fibrant objects
            are fibrant in this model structure.
          %h3#compatibility_with_other_models_for_1categories Compatibility with other models for (∞,1)-categories
          %p
            One often uses the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} hammock localization
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mi H
                    %mo :
                    %mi RelCat
                    %mo →
                    %mi sSetCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^H : RelCat \to sSetCat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sSetCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSetCat
            is given the Bergner model structure whose weak equivalences are the Dwyer-Kan equivalences: i.e. the local weak homotopy equivalences.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi RelCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RelCat
              is a Rezk equivalence iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^H(f)
              is a Dwyer-Kan equivalence.
          %p
            This is main theorem 1.4 of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#RezkIsDK"} Barwick-Kan
          %p
            The idea underlying marked simplicial is directly analogous to the idea underlying relative categories. In fact, the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi RelCat
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi sSet
                    %mo +
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi NC
                  %mo ,
                  %mi NW
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RelCat \to sSet^+ : (C,W) \to (NC, NW)
            preserves and reflects equivalences, since
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              be a fibrant replacement in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi sSetCat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSetCat
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi NC
                    %mo ,
                    %mi NW
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %msup
                      %mi NRL
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi W
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ♮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (NC, NW) \mapsto NRL^H(C,W)^\natural
              of marked simplicial sets is a natural weak equivalence.
          %p
            This is theorem 1.2.1 of
            %a{:href => "#Hinich13"} Hinich 13
          %h3#nerve_functors Nerve functors
          %p The compatibility of the various nerve and simplicial localization functors is in section 1.11 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clark+Barwick"} Clark Barwick
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Kan"} Dan Kan
              %em A characterization of simplicial localization functors
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1012.1540"}> arXiv:math/1012.1540
              )
          %h2#references References
          %ul#Meier
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clark+Barwick"} Clark Barwick
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Kan"} Dan Kan
              %ul
                %li#BarKan
                  %p
                    %em Relative categories: another model for the homotopy theory of homotopy theories
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1011.1691"}> arXiv:math/1011.1691
                    )
                %li
                  %p
                    %em A characterization of simplicial localization functors
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1012.1540"}> arXiv:math/1012.1540
                    )
                %li#RezkIsDK
                  %p
                    %em In the category of relative categories the Rezk equivalences are exactly the DK-equivalences
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1012.1541"}> arXiv:math/1012.1541
                    )
                %li
                  %p
                    %em A Thomason-like Quillen equivalence between quasi-categories and relative categories
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1101.0772"}> arXiv:math/1101.0772
                    )
                %li
                  %p
                    %em Partial model categories and their simplicial nerves
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1102.2512"}> arXiv:math/1102.2512
                    )
            %li#Hinich13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Hinich"} Vladimir Hinich
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Dwyer-Kan Localization Revisited
              %p
                Homology, Homotopy and Applications Volume 18 (2016) Number 1 (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.4128"}> arXiv:1311.4128
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.4310/HHA.2016.v18.n1.a3"} doi:10.4310/HHA.2016.v18.n1.a3
            %li
              %p
                Lennart Meier,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fibration Categories are Fibrant Relative Categories
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.02036"} arxiv
